// Why a decode stopped, and the one distinction the shipped decoder cannot draw.
//
// `issues/system/closed/0102` is about exactly this and closed on the smallest honest move: the
// promise was withdrawn, and `gunzipStream` now says in a comment that *"a source failure and a
// corrupt archive are indistinguishable from here"*. Its resolution then wrote down the shape a real
// fix would have:
//
//   > **`inflateInto` returns a result rather than trapping on a source failure only** — corruption
//   > keeps trapping — and every read site propagates it. That is a refactor of the decode loop, not
//   > a line.
//
// That is a description of `try`. What made it a refactor is threading a status back through every
// symbol read by hand; the whole content of `try` is not doing that. So this package's headline is
// not that the vision design is nicer — it is that a filed issue's stated cost is the thing the
// language removes, and the decision it was declined on ("it should be done for a reason better
// than closing an argument about an unreachable branch") is untouched. **The price changes, not the
// answer.**

// Nothing is imported. `union<A, B>` is a **type form**, like `T[]` and `T?`, not a name that
// lives in a module — and three files in this directory imported it from `"core"` before a check
// over every vision import against every vision export said so. It is the sort of thing that is
// invisible while nothing resolves imports, and obvious the moment something does.

/** The source stopped producing bytes and said why. Nothing is wrong with the archive. */
export struct SourceFailed { string why; }

/** The member is not gzip, or is gzip and is wrong. */
export struct BadMagic { i32 saw; }
export struct BadMethod { i32 cm; }
export struct BadBlockType { i32 btype; }
export struct BadHuffmanCode { i32 at; }
export struct BadDistance { i32 distance; i32 available; }
export struct Truncated { string field; }

/** The bytes decoded and are not the bytes that were compressed. */
export struct ChecksumMismatch { u32 want; u32 got; }
export struct LengthMismatch { i32 want; i32 got; }

/**
 * Everything a decode can answer other than bytes.
 *
 * **Two groups, and keeping them one union is the decision.** `SourceFailed` is somebody else's
 * disk; every other member is a statement about the archive. `0102` wants them told apart and the
 * shipped decoder cannot, which is the whole issue — so a fault set that merges them again would be
 * reproducing the bug with better syntax.
 *
 * They are one union because they are one *return*: a caller writing to disk asks the same question
 * of both — did this finish — and only then asks which. What separates them is not the type, it is
 * that `SourceFailed` carries no claim about the data, so a caller may retry it and may not retry
 * the others. `Corrupt` below is the name for that half, and it is the arm most callers want.
 */
export union<SourceFailed, Corrupt> Fault;

export union<
  BadMagic, BadMethod, BadBlockType, BadHuffmanCode, BadDistance, Truncated,
  ChecksumMismatch, LengthMismatch
> Corrupt;
